# narrative (0.17.0 N1): the Codex's volumes (narrative 1.3, 1.4, 1.7) and the cabin logs of the buildings 0.16.0 has
# volumes: <n>.title, the text (\n is a line break), <n>.when for a volume that opens later
fluxecho.codex.vol.0.title=Volume 0 · The Crossing
fluxecho.codex.vol.0=The people of the homeland said that every world floats on the same sea.\n\nThat sea has no water. It is flux: it runs beneath the ground of every world and carries power, memory and matter from one shore to another. It has five layers. Energy lies at the top; below it, echoes; below that, shards; then manifestation; and at the very bottom, the source: a seam that opens onto whatever lies outside the sea.\n\nIn the homeland, those who took ships across the sea were called ferrymen, and a ferryman's ship was called a nexus. Under way, a nexus is a ship; at rest, it is an anchor.\n\n§7[Ship's log · fragment] ...spring tide... third layer lost pressure... fourth layer lost pressure... abandoning...§r\n\nYou are a ferryman. You know these things the way you know your own hands. But you do not remember the ship's name, or which shore you were bound for, or anything before that night. Only the tide: the sea turning over from below, and the nexus's five layers stripped away one by one, like the shades lifted off a lamp one after another until only the wick was left.\n\nYou woke on a strange shore. Beside you lay that wick: a cold crystal core that flickers now and then. The wick still remembers what a door looked like as it broke.
fluxecho.codex.vol.1.title=Volume I · Aground
fluxecho.codex.vol.1=Day 1 aground. The core is still warm. It lights up every four seconds, as if counting heartbeats. I built it a shell of sand and copper sheet. It says nothing.\n\nDay 9 aground. This shore has kept a whole craft of burning coal, smelting copper and driving everything with steam. I follow it. The furnace is hard to please, but the fire is real.\n\nDay 23 aground. Beside a boiler I opened a pinhole, as thin as a needle. I put my hand to it: there is a sea underneath. I pushed steam into it, and the steam went everywhere the pinhole reaches. So here, too, the sea carries things for people.\n\nDay 40 aground. Shards lie on the sea floor; every vein of ore on this shore is the shadow one of them casts upward. I built a machine to dredge the shadows. Besides the ore, it brings up a grey-blue powder.\n\nI know this colour. It is the nexus's ash. It sank to the floor of this sea and mixed with the shards of this shore: fifteen ores in a hundred carry it.\n\nThen I will dredge it up, one ore at a time.
fluxecho.codex.vol.2.title=Volume II · Echoes
fluxecho.codex.vol.2=This world remembers.\n\nThe day I first took a bee's imprint with my own hands, a new line appeared in the sea. Faint, but there: the next day, and the hundredth day. The monsters I killed myself, the aspects I learned myself, the blood orbs I made myself, each left a line like that in the sea. I call it an echo.\n\nAn echo only takes what is real. Imprint paper cannot fool it; copies cannot fool it. But once it remembers, I can build a small machine and hand the thing over to it to repeat, amplifying the line with power. The first time must be by hand. After that, the machine.\n\nI begin to see why this shore is so strange. Bees, magic, blood, books that turn their own pages, the other planets up in the sky... none of them grew here. Like me, they drifted in from other shores.\n\nThe core glowed for a long time today. I held it to my ear:\n\n§b[...course... lost. Memory layer... writable. Borrowing... local echoes...]§r\n\nIt is using this world's memory to remember itself, a little at a time.\n\nI remembered something too: in the homeland, the sky turns gold at dusk.
fluxecho.codex.vol.2.when=This volume opens the first time this world remembers something you did by hand: imprint a bee, learn an aspect, kill a monster with a blank imprint on you... One entry in the Echo Codex is enough.
fluxecho.codex.vol.3.title=Volume III · Anchoring
fluxecho.codex.vol.3=§b[Evenstar] Rekindling complete. Keel: none. Cabins: none. Sails: none.§r\n§b[Evenstar] Leap gate: broken. Remaining: two piers, two horns.§r\n§b[Evenstar] I remember how it looked when it broke. I will raise it as it broke, first.§r\n§b[Evenstar] You have set me down on land.§r\n\n"If we cannot go back, we anchor first."\n\n§b[Evenstar] Anchoring is not a sailing order.§r\n\n"Then take it as one."\n\n§b[Evenstar] ...Acknowledged. First sailing order: anchor.§r\n§b[Evenstar] I remember the original plans. The log cabin lies beside the keel, the life cabin on the sunward side, the engine room right beneath me, the cargo hold low in the belly. I remember where every cabin goes. Only where.§r\n\n"Then follow the plans. Build a ship on land."\n\nIt says its name is Evenstar. It says I gave it that name, in the homeland, long ago: the evening star is the first to light up at dusk, and when a ferryman sees it, they know it is time to go home. I do not remember. But it sounds like a name I would give.\n\n§b[Evenstar] Tide record: full moon, midnight, +20%. Inference: the tide's source is not on this side. Something on the far side of the sea is pulling.§r\n\n"The homeland?"\n\n§b[Evenstar] Uncertain. But at every spring tide, it is closest to us.§r
fluxecho.codex.vol.3.when=This volume opens the first time your Flux Nexus forms (the Anchoring research is done with it).

# cabin logs: the first line is the log's heading (the phase year it belongs to and the building), then the city's record
fluxecho.codex.hall.nexus=Year of Phase I · Flux Nexus\nThe keel, and the mainmast too. The core was set down and Evenstar built from the plans: the frame first, then the crystal seat; the core floats in the ring and a pillar of light goes straight up. Each phase adds a stage. On the day of the leap, the bearing home is set in its crystal seat, and the order to sail goes out from here.\nIf we cannot go back, we anchor first.
fluxecho.codex.hall.library=Year of Phase I · Echo Archive\nThe log cabin. A long hall on two floors: seventy-eight shelves, seven hundred and two books. Each book is a thing done once by hand; written onto an index card, the city's machines do it after. Before the leap, every record is to be bound into one Traveller's Book.\nEach book is a thing done once by hand.
fluxecho.codex.hall.eco_dome=Year of Phase I · Eco Dome\nThe life cabin. A dome of glass, floating islands round a tree of light, twelve planters where bees, trees, butterflies and seeds each have their place. At the leap, it carries an ark of seeds, each one imprinted by hand.\nWhen we go home, we take a little of this world's life with us.
fluxecho.codex.hall.root=Year of Phase II · Tidal Well\nThe engine room, right under the nexus. A shaft twenty-six blocks deep; three rings of light rise and fall with the tide, highest at midnight under a full moon. In the year of Phase IV the well went deeper and grew into the Deep Root: eight bays in a root chamber, conduits running with light. Before the leap, it stores a shipload of power.\nAt midnight under a full moon the homeland is closest to this ship, and the well eats its fill.
fluxecho.codex.hall.dome_generator=Year of Phase II · Echo Dome Generator\nThe hull. A dish of a pod on six curved legs, forty blocks over the nexus, holds up a dome you can barely see: under it nothing spawns, no blast harms the city, no arrow gets in. In the year of Phase IV the pod let go of its legs and floated. At the leap, it closes the whole city into one sphere.\nWhen the wind rises at sea, a ship needs a hull.
fluxecho.codex.hall.dual_foil_vault=Year of Phase II · Dual-foil Vault\nThe cargo hold. A windowless monolith, its whole front one great painting; inside, a gallery of forty-two canvases and one dual foil on the back wall. What goes in becomes a picture; peeled off, it stands up as itself again. Evenstar said: first learn to load cargo; the last cargo is the city.\nThings go in as a sheet of paper and come out as they were.
fluxecho.codex.hall.mana_fountain=Year of Phase II · Mana Fountain Array\nThe law of life, from another shore. A cloister round a court: a fountain in the middle, a magic circle on the ground, and urns holding the flowers of the far side. That is a world bound together by a world tree; the array is a root of it reaching onto this shore.\nBranches were always the roads between worlds.
fluxecho.codex.hall.arcane_court=Year of Phase II · Arcane Court\nThe law of reason, from another shore. Under a spire, an octagonal arcane hall: an infusion circle, cauldrons, pillars of aspects, research desks. Aspects are the letters another world writes matter in. Before the leap, the team must know forty of them.\nTo describe a place, first learn its letters.
fluxecho.codex.hall.resonance_gate=Year of Phase II · Resonance Gate\nThe bow, and the city's gate. Two wings astride the avenue, a bridge between them, six columns in the resonance chamber on the bridge; over it a crystal spire hangs and slowly turns. Before the leap, the six columns are tuned to the homeland's pitch.\nThe way home is a pitch.
fluxecho.codex.hall.sophon_array=Year of Phase III · Sophon Array\nThe ship's brain. A lattice sphere on a podium round a point of light; given power, the point unfolds into a turning icosahedron and at night spreads a sky of circuits. Forty-eight racks, three samples each.\nIt has a second mind now; the course is reckoned from this night on.\n§b[Evenstar] Do you miss home?§r
fluxecho.codex.hall.holo_table=Year of Phase III · Holo Sand Table\nThe bridge. A round hall under a shallow dome; on the round table the whole city turns as a hologram, nine consoles round it. Before the leap, the little city on the table makes the voyage first.\nThe order to sail is given here.
fluxecho.codex.hall.blood_covenant=Year of Phase II · Blood Covenant Altar\nThe law of the pact, from another shore. A red altar in three tiers round an open oculus, an orb hanging under it; the orb cradles and ritual stones hold the team's blood. Blood is the signature of who you are. Before the leap, everyone who goes signs the pact once at the altar.\nThe seam must know you.
fluxecho.codex.hall.hunting_ground=Year of Phase II · Echo Hunting Ground\nThe law of the wild, from another shore. An open amphitheatre round a holo arena, twelve imprint consoles. The monsters of this shore drifted in too: they are fellow travellers. Before the leap, the bestiary must hold the Ender Dragon and the Wither.\nThey drifted here too.
fluxecho.codex.hall.observatory=Year of Phase IV · Observatory\nThe lookout, on a floating platform. A stepped dome opened by a meridian slit, a telescope in the observing room, a ring lens floating over the dome. Before the leap, the seven stars of the Dipper are fixed here.\n§b[Evenstar] That is where my name comes from.§r
fluxecho.codex.hall.sky_sails=Year of Phase IV · Sky Sail Array\nThe sails, on a floating platform. A bridge cabin and six masts in three pairs, three golden sails of light spread toward the sun. At the leap, the three sails open to three times their size and turn to the zenith.\nThe light the sea folds upward, caught in a sail.
fluxecho.codex.hall.weather_station=Year of Phase IV · Weather Station\nThe weather, on a floating platform. An instrument hut and a weather mast; cups and vanes turn under a churning cloud that thickens before rain. On the night of the leap, it opens the window for the ship.\nIt picks the night you put to sea.
fluxecho.codex.hall.droplet_hangar=Year of Phase IV · Droplet Hangar\nThe escort, on a floating platform. A control kiosk in the middle, eight cradles round it, a mirror droplet over each with the city in its mirror. Before the leap, the eight droplets fall into formation.\nIt keeps the deck swept for you.
